.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_KDF-HKDF 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_KDF-HKDF
.Nd The HKDF EVP_KDF implementation
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Support for computing the
.Sy HKDF
KDF through the
.Sy EVP_KDF
API.
.Pp
The EVP_KDF-HKDF algorithm implements the HKDF key derivation function.
HKDF follows the "extract-then-expand" paradigm, where the KDF logically
consists of two modules.
The first stage takes the input keying material and "extracts" from it a
fixed-length pseudorandom key K. The second stage "expands" the key K
into several additional pseudorandom keys (the output of the KDF).
.Pp
The output is considered to be keying material.
.Ss Identity
"HKDF" is the name for this implementation; it can be used with the
.Xr EVP_KDF_fetch 3
function.
.Ss Supported parameters
The supported parameters are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqproperties" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_PROPERTIES Ns ) <UTF8 string>
.It \(dqdigest" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_DIGEST Ns ) <UTF8 string>
.It \(dqkey" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_KEY Ns ) <octet string>
.It \(dqsalt" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_SALT Ns ) <octet string>
These parameters work as described in
.Xr EVP_KDF 3 .
.It \(dqinfo" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_INFO Ns ) <octet string>
This parameter sets the info value.
The length of the context info buffer cannot exceed 1024 bytes; this
should be more than enough for any normal use of HKDF.
.It \(dqmode" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_MODE Ns ) <UTF8 string> or <integer>
This parameter sets the mode for the HKDF operation.
There are three modes that are currently defined:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqEXTRACT_AND_EXPAND" or Sy EVP_KDF_HKDF_MODE_EXTRACT_AND_EXPAND
This is the default mode.
Calling
.Xr EVP_KDF_derive 3
on an EVP_KDF_CTX set up for HKDF will perform an extract followed by an
expand operation in one go.
The derived key returned will be the result after the expand operation.
The intermediate fixed-length pseudorandom key K is not returned.
.Pp
In this mode the digest, key, salt and info values must be set before a
key is derived otherwise an error will occur.
.It \(dqEXTRACT_ONLY" or Sy EVP_KDF_HKDF_MODE_EXTRACT_ONLY
In this mode calling
.Xr EVP_KDF_derive 3
will just perform the extract operation.
The value returned will be the intermediate fixed-length pseudorandom
key K. The
.Em keylen
parameter must match the size of K, which can be looked up by calling
.Xr EVP_KDF_CTX_get_kdf_size 3
after setting the mode and digest.
.Pp
The digest, key and salt values must be set before a key is derived
otherwise an error will occur.
.It \(dqEXPAND_ONLY" or Sy EVP_KDF_HKDF_MODE_EXPAND_ONLY
In this mode calling
.Xr EVP_KDF_derive 3
will just perform the expand operation.
The input key should be set to the intermediate fixed-length
pseudorandom key K returned from a previous extract operation.
.Pp
The digest, key and info values must be set before a key is derived
otherwise an error will occur.
.El
.El
.Pp
The OpenSSL FIPS provider also supports the following parameters:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqfips-indicator" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_FIPS_APPROVED_INDICATOR Ns ) <integer>
A getter that returns 1 if the operation is FIPS approved, or 0
otherwise.
This may be used after calling EVP_KDF_derive.
It returns 0 if "key-check" is set to 0 and the check fails.
.It \(dqkey-check" ( Ns Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_FIPS_KEY_CHECK Ns ) <integer>
The default value of 1 causes an error during
.Xr EVP_KDF_CTX_set_params 3
if the length of used key-derivation key
.Pf ( Sy OSSL_KDF_PARAM_KEY )
is shorter than 112 bits.
Setting this to zero will ignore the error and set the approved
"fips-indicator" to 0. This option breaks FIPS compliance if it causes
the approved "fips-indicator" to return 0.
.El
.Sh NOTES
A context for HKDF can be obtained by calling:
.Bd -literal
 EVP_KDF *kdf = EVP_KDF_fetch(NULL, "HKDF", NULL);
 EVP_KDF_CTX *kctx = EVP_KDF_CTX_new(kdf);
.Ed
.Pp
The output length of an HKDF expand operation is specified via the
.Em keylen
parameter to the
.Xr EVP_KDF_derive 3
function.
When using EVP_KDF_HKDF_MODE_EXTRACT_ONLY the
.Em keylen
parameter must equal the size of the intermediate fixed-length
pseudorandom key otherwise an error will occur.
For that mode, the fixed output size can be looked up by calling
.Xr EVP_KDF_CTX_get_kdf_size 3
after setting the mode and digest on the
.Sy EVP_KDF_CTX .
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example derives 10 bytes using SHA-256 with the secret key
"secret", salt value "salt" and info value "label":
.Bd -literal
 EVP_KDF *kdf;
 EVP_KDF_CTX *kctx;
 unsigned char out[10];
 OSSL_PARAM params[5], *p = params;

 kdf = EVP_KDF_fetch(NULL, "HKDF", NULL);
 kctx = EVP_KDF_CTX_new(kdf);
 EVP_KDF_free(kdf);

 *p++ = OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_string(OSSL_KDF_PARAM_DIGEST,
                                         SN_sha256, strlen(SN_sha256));
 *p++ = OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_string(OSSL_KDF_PARAM_KEY,
                                          "secret", (size_t)6);
 *p++ = OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_string(OSSL_KDF_PARAM_INFO,
                                          "label", (size_t)5);
 *p++ = OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_string(OSSL_KDF_PARAM_SALT,
                                          "salt", (size_t)4);
 *p = OSSL_PARAM_construct_end();
 if (EVP_KDF_derive(kctx, out, sizeof(out), params) <= 0) {
     error("EVP_KDF_derive");
 }

 EVP_KDF_CTX_free(kctx);
.Ed
.Sh CONFORMING TO
RFC 5869
